+# Adjacent scalar retention: second bounded experiment for #39
+
+Status: experimental implementation, not accepted optimization. The archived
+binary-left-only experiment remains a rejected historical result. This candidate
+tests a different opportunity: ordinary scalar loads and constants also leave
+their result in `t0`, and postorder expression lowering often consumes the most
+recent result as the right operand.
+
+Implementer: Codex. The maintainer's 2026-10-03 #39 review waiver applies;
+compiler/checker agreement, frozen budgets and backend/CI gates remain required.
+
+## Machine contract fixed before implementation
+
+- Enable only in functions already admitted by the conservative scalar-slot
+  allocator, at optimization levels above zero. Resource, borrow, address-exposed,
+  wide-value and unclassified-call exclusions stay unchanged.
+- Retain at most one `(SSA value ID, t0)` fact between immediately adjacent
+  instructions in one basic block. Eligible producers are scalar `LoadConst`,
+  `LoadVar` and ordinary scalar `Binary` operations. All other instructions
+  invalidate the fact. Do not infer value identity from a reused stack offset.
+- Each eligible producer still writes its normal stack slot. These emitters
+  finish with the stored scalar value in `t0`; stack-address materialization
+  must preserve its source register. No dead-store removal is part of this test.
+- A consumer is an ordinary scalar binary operation. When its left operand is
+  retained, skip that reload. When its right operand is retained, move `t0` to
+  `t1` before loading a different left operand. If both operands name the retained
+  SSA value, the same move supplies the right operand. Preserve operand order
+  for subtraction, division, remainder, shifts and comparisons.
+- Loading another operand uses stack helpers with the retained register in the
+  explicit avoid set. Scalar immediate materialization changes its destination
+  only. Existing division/shift guards, normalization and result stores remain.
+- Calls and helpers inherit no retained-value guarantee. Joins, backedges,
+  entry blocks, terminators and fused branch emission start from the established
+  stack contract. No fact crosses a block or function boundary; outgoing stack
+  arguments and caller buffers remain governed by their existing ABI.
+- Do not repurpose scratch storage, merge parameter spill slots, alter witness
+  ownership, change source/proof attribution, or change the instruction set.
+
+## Acceptance experiment
+
+Compile and execute probes that hit left, right and duplicate operands, with
+noncommutative operations, overlapping live values, joins/backedges, calls,
+large stack offsets, outgoing arguments and fixed-width values. Demonstrate
+fallbacks as well as candidate hits, preserving exact success and rejection.
+Compare against the same compiler without this transformation, not merely an
+unoptimized build that differs in other passes.
+
+Measure static and executed loads/stores, deployed ELF bytes, authoritative
+group cycles and static/observed stack separately. Retain the nine scalar,
+41 expanded-policy, matched Rust, growth and multi-Script baseline corpora and
+their frozen ceilings. Recompute the frontier instead of adding estimated
+savings to historical results. Actual checked bundle bytes and independently
+rebound machine mutations must retain their existing evidence boundaries.
+
+Adopt only if the measured benefit justifies this small emitter contract and
+the complete backend and CI gates pass. Otherwise archive the patch and exact
+measurements as a rejected experiment. Neither outcome admits a general
+register allocator or helper-scratch reuse.
